The shown function `io_register_zcrx_ifq()` contains the vulnerable post-open error path. After allocating `ifq` and calling `io_allocate_rbuf_ring(ifq, &reg, &rd)`, any later failure jumps to `err:` and calls `io_zcrx_ifq_free(ifq);` directly: `err:
	io_zcrx_ifq_free(ifq);
	return ret;`. That free path immediately does `io_free_rbuf_ring(ifq);` and `kfree(ifq);`, i.e. it destroys the context rather than unregistering it through the normal teardown path. The normal unregister helper shown below is `io_unregister_zcrx_ifqs(struct io_ring_ctx *ctx)`, which first clears `ctx->ifq = NULL;` and then frees the object, but the error path in `io_register_zcrx_ifq()` does not use this teardown. Since the vulnerability root cause is exactly the post-open error path freeing the zcrx context directly instead of unregistering it, this commit version still has the bug and does not contain the fix.
